courses.learning_outcomes:
After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) acquire a solid understanding in applied game theory;
(2) appreciate the application of game theory in business;
(3) apply game-theoretic analysis, both formally and intuitively, to various interaction in business environment.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) explain the key concepts, techniques, and practices of web analytics and intelligence;
(2) master the techniques that can successfully advocate and promote the use of web analytics;
(3) use web analytics tools (such as Google Analytics) to evaluate web usage and web performance for website optimization; and
(4) apply web analytics skills and abilities to solve business problems, enhance business decisions, and improve business intelligence.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) Make good managerial decisions in the modern business landscape with scarce resources, competitive pressures, and government oversight;
(2) Master the various analytical tools in managerial economics;
(3) Understand the business models of selected new industries in the information age;
(4) Read and interpret popular and trade press in business from the perspective of economics.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) gain a thorough understanding of the existing state of economic knowledge on the topic they select;
(2) conduct a critical analysis of corporate issues by economic framework;
(3) write a comprehensive research report on corporate issues.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) understand the information systems development life cycle & IS expenditure planning;
(2) recognize two major concepts & methodologies used in business processes analysis;
(3) master the methods and techniques in requirement analysis and specification;
(4) understand some IS design/testing issues
(5) explain the key methodologies and mindsets in IS world.

• Master the fundamental terminology and concepts of data communication;
• Master the fundamental terminology and concepts of computer networking;
• Discuss the application/impact/issues of data communications and networking in common business applications and management;
• Make intelligent, reasonable, thoughtful, and accurate decisions about the optimal configuration, installation, and operation of a data communication network.

After completing this course, students should be able to:

* Apply economic theories and quantitative methods to real-world aviation cases, enabling data-driven decision-making in airline and airport management;
* Analyze the economic structure and competitive dynamics of the airline and airport industries;
* Assess the effects of government regulations on the performance and competitiveness of the aviation industry.

Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

(1) formulate the topic of a project that address a complex aviation management issue;

(2) apply empirical methods to analyze industry challenges;

(3) integrate data and knowledge to provide evidence-based recommendations; and

(4) communicate effectively in the problem, process, results and recommendations of the project in a professional manner, using written report and oral presentation.

Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

(1) Evaluate the regulatory frameworks, functions, operations, business models and business strategies of global airlines;

(2) Analyse key factors, benefits and challenges of smart technologies in airline operations towards improving safety and enhancing passenger experience;

(3) Apply pricing strategies, revenue optimization and diversification, cost control and cost reduction, and financial management in airline management;

(4) Apply strategic management concepts and analytical tools to the airline industry; and

(5) Appraise airline strategies driven by competition, changes in regulations and market environment, customer expectations, environmental issues and other contemporary issues.

Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

(1) Analyse the regulatory frameworks, economic, social and environmental issues in airport operations, management and strategies;

(2) Analyse the airport systems, components, operations and management of airside and landside to enhance operational efficiency and aviation safety;

(3) Evaluate the airport ecosystem, impact of stakeholders and the importance in global economy;

(4) Examine global threats and risks, and relevant preventative measures associated with airport management; and

(5) Critically analyze the roles and responsibilities of major stakeholders in airport business continuity management and crisis management framework.

Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

(1) Evaluate the conceptual framework of financial management in the air transport industry;

(2) Evaluate financial indicators, financial performance and profit maximization of airlines and airport operators;

(3) Develop financial forecast for organizations in the global air transport industry;

(4) Analyse the trend and structure in aircraft leasing towards aircraft portfolio management; and

(5) Critically evaluate air traffic forecasts, aircraft valuation, aircraft lessor risk and aircraft risk portfolio.

After completion of the seminar course , students are expected:
1. To be familiar with the literature in their own research area;
2. To be able to perform review of the most recent literature;
3. To be able to develop new research topics;
4. To be better prepared for thesis writing in a scientific way.

After completing the course, students should be able to:
(1) understand the fundamental concepts of data communication and computer networking;
(2) master the application and management of data communications and networking in business applications;
(3) formulate the policies and governance of an organization’s IT infrastructure, networks, and business operating environment; and
(4) make intelligent, reasonable, thoughtful, and accurate decisions about the optimal configuration, installation, security and operation of a data communication network.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) understand the legal system in Hong Kong;
(2) have a sufficient understanding of contract law and tort law and IT law to handle the common situations arising in the business world;
(3) avoid the legal traps in their daily personal and business encounters;
(4) obtain a sufficient knowledge as to various aspects on the above from a PRC perspective.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) have a working knowledge of core forecasting-related topics on intermediate-level econometric theory;
(2) apply and compare a number of forecasting techniques, such as multiple regression, smoothing, decomposition, and ARIMA models;
(3) develop data handling skills, using specialized software;
(4) have a solid understanding of how to interpret and report forecast results;
(5) read, understand and interpret computerized econometric output.
